1
00:00:05,353 --> 00:00:15,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Majjhima Nikaya 108, called Gopaka Moggallāna.

2
00:00:17,878 --> 00:00:26,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's when Venerable Ananda was living in the squirrel sanctuary, not long after the Blessed One had attained final nibbāna.

3
00:00:28,202 --> 00:00:29,463
[SPEAKER_00]: The Brahmin continues to ask,

4
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:36,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Master Nanda, Master Gautama was living at Vesali, in the hall of the peak roof in the great wood.

5
00:00:36,512 --> 00:00:41,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I went there and approached the Master, and in many ways, he gave a talk about meditation.

6
00:00:42,135 --> 00:00:47,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Master Gautama was a meditator, cultivated meditation, and he praised every type of meditation.

7
00:00:48,697 --> 00:00:49,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Every type of meditation.

8
00:00:50,658 --> 00:00:51,018
[SPEAKER_01]: He did.

9
00:00:52,039 --> 00:00:52,979
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't.

10
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:59,342
[SPEAKER_00]: The Blessed One did not praise every type of meditation, nor did he condemn every type of meditation.

11
00:01:01,682 --> 00:01:13,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only meditation you praised was a meditation of jhānas, or being able to be quite secluded from sensual pleasures, one then meditates.

12
00:01:14,104 --> 00:01:14,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

13
00:01:14,464 --> 00:01:17,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Meditation is about secluding yourself from sensual pleasures.

14
00:01:18,825 --> 00:01:29,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding the extent of the sensual domain, the depth of sense desire, and surmounting it.

15
00:01:30,203 --> 00:01:34,224
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just in the sense of, oh, I don't have any desire right now, so I'm ready to meditate.

16
00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:40,826
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, in other sutras, the Buddha would say, a monk would review his mind, like, is there a central desire in me now?

17
00:01:41,546 --> 00:01:42,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no.

18
00:01:43,606 --> 00:01:50,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Next question then is, well, are there things in me now, on account of which a future central desire can manifest?

19
00:01:51,188 --> 00:01:54,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't just stop by, okay, I don't have any desire, so I'm fine.

20
00:01:54,569 --> 00:01:54,729
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

21
00:01:55,129 --> 00:01:58,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I don't have any particular desire, but am I still liable to desire?

22
00:01:59,912 --> 00:02:12,295
[SPEAKER_01]: If things change, if something comes along within my senses, could I not have sense... would I guarantee that I will not have sensual desire arising in me, because I abandon everything that's a necessary basis for that sensuality?

23
00:02:13,616 --> 00:02:14,276
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I'm not sure.

24
00:02:14,356 --> 00:02:15,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, there you go, that's your work.

25
00:02:16,616 --> 00:02:22,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding the extent of your sensual desire, and understanding things on account of which sensual desire arises,

26
00:02:24,369 --> 00:02:29,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things that you carry here and now, not like when, oh, in the future, when sensual desire arises, deal with it.

27
00:02:29,192 --> 00:02:29,352
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

28
00:02:29,372 --> 00:02:40,960
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would say, before it's manifested, you want to discern things in your mind, that are there, on account of which future sensual desire will be manifested.

29
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:41,780
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that?

30
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:48,104
[SPEAKER_01]: That main... Well, the main reason for that is the lack of context, lack of the perspective.

31
00:02:49,245 --> 00:02:53,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, as the Buddha said, improper ayoniso, manasikāra.

32
00:02:54,879 --> 00:03:07,569
[SPEAKER_01]: There is not a single thing that would bring un-arisen sensual desire to arise and the arisen one to increase as improper or that ayoniso manasikāra.

33
00:03:07,609 --> 00:03:18,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, loss of perspective on account of your own existence, situation, that's the sole reason why sensuality would increase or arise again.

34
00:03:19,736 --> 00:03:21,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would be the main loss of perspective?

35
00:03:21,817 --> 00:03:25,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, paying attention to the sign of the beautifulism.

36
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:34,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, see, that's already... paying that type of attention, it's already the result of you losing the perspective on a more fundamental level.

37
00:03:35,306 --> 00:03:38,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yoniso manasikāra is not like proper attention.

38
00:03:38,568 --> 00:03:44,352
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, you're attending to this, you focus on certain things, and then everything else gets excluded.

39
00:03:46,340 --> 00:03:53,105
[SPEAKER_01]: As we discussed before, yoniso manasikāra is basically the root, the origin of whatever you're attending.

40
00:03:53,145 --> 00:04:04,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I'm attending beautiful objects in the world, or if I'm attending ugly objects in the world, yoniso, aspect of that manasikāra, of that attention, is my perspective on the whole situation.

41
00:04:04,854 --> 00:04:06,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It's where my attention is rooted.

42
00:04:06,435 --> 00:04:15,042
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you would be attending to the beautiful signs, for example, because your perspective sends you that way.

43
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Your perspective forgets the danger of attending to beautiful.

44
00:04:19,762 --> 00:04:21,823
[SPEAKER_01]: It forgets the peril of sensuality.

45
00:04:21,863 --> 00:04:26,846
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, next thing you know, you're attending to beautiful signs, because you forgot, you lost the context.

46
00:04:26,946 --> 00:04:27,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

47
00:04:27,766 --> 00:04:30,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what yoniso manasikāra is.

48
00:04:30,048 --> 00:04:34,010
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that fundamental perspective that then directs your attention this way or that way.

49
00:04:34,890 --> 00:04:43,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why there isn't such a single thing that could bring sensuality to increase and non-arisen one to arise, as loss of that perspective.

50
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:45,414
[SPEAKER_01]: On that fundamental level.

51
00:04:46,014 --> 00:05:08,349
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, if you never completely lose perspective on the fact that sensuality is dangerous, no word engaging in, pleasure of enunciation is much greater if you develop it, even... doesn't matter how many sensual objects might come your way, that perspective will keep your attention in check, so you will not attending... you will not be attending carelessly to these aspects that would then take you into sensual desire and sensual acts.

52
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:12,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in itself is already meditation.

53
00:05:14,385 --> 00:05:17,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when one wants to meditate, that's... that's what meditation is, basically.

54
00:05:18,346 --> 00:05:26,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing the strength of your mind, that will take you away from sensuality, not maintain you within it, and then make it grow further.

55
00:05:28,327 --> 00:05:30,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, we discussed that in a number of talks.

56
00:05:30,728 --> 00:05:33,028
[SPEAKER_01]: People can find them in that jhāna playlist.

57
00:05:34,688 --> 00:05:43,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the first jhāna, the way it's described, says, withdrawn from sensuality, from unwholesome states, you experience joy and pleasure on account of withdrawal.

58
00:05:45,023 --> 00:05:47,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, your mind is established in the jhāna.

59
00:05:48,148 --> 00:05:50,749
[SPEAKER_01]: But somehow, that got perverted in a course of time.

60
00:05:51,589 --> 00:06:05,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, people think is, okay, I'll just say no temporarily to my desires, do a meditation technique, get this special, super refined, pleasurable experience, and that's my jhāna.

61
00:06:06,152 --> 00:06:10,353
[SPEAKER_01]: As if, like, chasing the pleasure of your focusing is the jhāna practice.

62
00:06:11,013 --> 00:06:16,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the Buddha said that pleasure that people chase on account of jhāna, it's actually pleasure of renouncing the sensuality.

63
00:06:16,475 --> 00:06:17,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, jhāna is the result of that.

64
00:06:20,076 --> 00:06:41,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, jhāna follows only later, once you have been withdrawn from centrality, through and through, as it says in that sutta, knowing the gratification, the escape from it, your liability, you abandon the basis that makes you liable to centrality, through understanding, through discernment, through not losing the context, because you become more and more familiar with that right perspective.

65
00:06:42,130 --> 00:06:47,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will experience joy on account of being withdrawn, fundamentally,

66
00:06:48,078 --> 00:06:50,339
[SPEAKER_01]: From that sensual... from sensual domain in general.

67
00:06:50,399 --> 00:06:52,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Not this particular sense desire or that.

68
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:54,441
[SPEAKER_01]: In general, sense desire as a whole.

69
00:06:55,061 --> 00:06:56,422
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's extremely pleasant.

70
00:06:56,882 --> 00:07:02,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And that pleasure is something that your craving cannot take up, because you haven't done it through your action.

71
00:07:03,865 --> 00:07:07,867
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the result of you withdrawing from from all the pleasures you know.

72
00:07:08,267 --> 00:07:09,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasures of sensuality.

73
00:07:10,628 --> 00:07:12,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by being withdrawn from pleasure of sensuality,

74
00:07:14,397 --> 00:07:23,363
[SPEAKER_01]: The side effect of that is pleasure of renunciation, which is why the Buddha said you don't have to fear that pleasure of renunciation, because it's not underlined by craving, cannot be underlined by craving.

75
00:07:24,604 --> 00:07:26,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And the same said for the jhāna then.

76
00:07:26,985 --> 00:07:32,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So that pleasure of renunciation, if you cultivate, your mind then gets established in the first jhāna.

77
00:07:33,269 --> 00:07:37,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So first is renunciation, understanding sensual domain, pleasure on account of it.

78
00:07:37,912 --> 00:07:38,813
[SPEAKER_01]: That might take, you know,

79
00:07:42,086 --> 00:07:52,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, jhāna is even a further symptom.

80
00:07:53,973 --> 00:08:01,037
[SPEAKER_01]: A subsequent symptom that comes on account of you being withdrawn from sensuality and dwelling, as the Buddha would say, in that state.

81
00:08:01,097 --> 00:08:05,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you go to the toilet, extend your arm, eat, stand, lay down, walk,

82
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:09,441
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be abiding in that divine state.

83
00:08:10,242 --> 00:08:14,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I think I can meditate before that...

84
00:08:15,203 --> 00:08:15,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

85
00:08:15,604 --> 00:08:18,105
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be meditating within the sensual domain.

86
00:08:18,265 --> 00:08:19,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it would have meant.

87
00:08:19,585 --> 00:08:21,266
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be meditating with sensuality.

88
00:08:22,507 --> 00:08:23,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not incidental.

89
00:08:24,348 --> 00:08:25,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in that sutta.

90
00:08:26,909 --> 00:08:35,514
[SPEAKER_01]: In another sutta that uses the same description of meditating with sensuality, but then it also gives the similes of how would you meditate with sensuality.

91
00:08:36,374 --> 00:08:41,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And each and every simile, basically, what it has in common is a focusing practice.

92
00:08:43,838 --> 00:08:50,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Like praying upon a sort of... whatever.

93
00:08:50,721 --> 00:08:51,942
[SPEAKER_01]: A cat prays upon a mouse.

94
00:08:52,862 --> 00:08:54,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, what are you... you're trying to get something.

95
00:08:55,483 --> 00:08:56,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the point, that's the point.

96
00:08:56,804 --> 00:09:09,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are not withdrawn from sensual domain, on account of which you then experience the pleasure and then jhāna develops, you... whatever effort you make will be rooted in the sensual domain.

97
00:09:09,989 --> 00:09:11,050
[SPEAKER_01]: In trying to get pleasure.

98
00:09:11,842 --> 00:09:12,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

99
00:09:12,202 --> 00:09:22,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily think about pleasure, in thinking that you can perform things towards the right meditation, as opposed to understand things that will make you withdrawn from them.

100
00:09:23,253 --> 00:09:29,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the whole point of samādhi is viveka from unwholesome, literally, seclusion from unwholesome states.

101
00:09:31,188 --> 00:09:34,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't do that on account of your unwholesome state.

102
00:09:34,311 --> 00:09:38,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only withdraw yourself by undoing, basically, your lack of perspective.

103
00:09:39,577 --> 00:09:42,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are within unwholesome because you don't have perspective on it.

104
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:44,863
[SPEAKER_01]: You start developing perspective through contemplating unwholesome.

105
00:09:45,954 --> 00:09:48,816
[SPEAKER_01]: The gratification, the danger, the escape in regard to desire.

106
00:09:49,137 --> 00:09:49,617
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how.

107
00:09:49,637 --> 00:09:59,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, by focusing on this, and after three seconds I do this, and then I move that, and then I watch this step, and then the fourth step, and then I look at this moment, and then that moment, and then the next moment.

108
00:09:59,706 --> 00:10:05,531
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you laying out what you're gonna do fully within on account of your sensual desires already.

109
00:10:06,332 --> 00:10:10,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even the hope of practicing the dhamma is rooted in sensuality for you.

110
00:10:10,622 --> 00:10:13,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're going that way of sensual development.

111
00:10:13,905 --> 00:10:18,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha did not go into specifics of, like, what you would be doing on account of sensuality.

112
00:10:19,031 --> 00:10:23,095
[SPEAKER_01]: He just said, unless you abandon the sensual domain, you will be meditating with sensuality.

113
00:10:23,215 --> 00:10:23,515
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

114
00:10:23,615 --> 00:10:26,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he said, like a cat waiting for a mouse.

115
00:10:28,621 --> 00:10:30,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a bird waiting for a fish.

116
00:10:31,263 --> 00:10:36,707
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm focusing, concentrating on one point, where the mouse will show itself, and then I'll pounce.

117
00:10:37,228 --> 00:10:38,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I will get it.

118
00:10:39,069 --> 00:10:44,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I will grab that jhāna, and I will have that supreme pleasure of jhāna, and I'll be an arahant.

119
00:10:45,654 --> 00:10:48,836
[SPEAKER_01]: And, on account of that, you're not... you're even further away from jhāna.

120
00:10:49,717 --> 00:11:00,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're chasing it as pleasure, failing to see the pleasure is secondary product of your withdrawal from sensuality, and then the jhāna is tertiary product of that.

121
00:11:02,165 --> 00:11:06,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Having abandoned sensuality, he experienced joy on account of that withdrawal.

122
00:11:08,089 --> 00:11:17,074
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in that state of joyous contentment of renunciation, his mind enters and dwells, abides, in that state of jhāna.

123
00:11:18,295 --> 00:11:18,575
[SPEAKER_00]: After.

124
00:11:20,636 --> 00:11:29,821
[SPEAKER_00]: You're saying about... you have to understand what sensuality is, the whole... you have to understand... Yeah, there is no third option, by the way.

125
00:11:29,961 --> 00:11:30,642
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either not

126
00:11:31,790 --> 00:11:39,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding sensuality, which means you're meditating with it, and all your efforts revolve around it, or you're making the effort to undo your wrong views in regard to your own desires.

127
00:11:40,856 --> 00:11:44,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so, the nature of those desires will become apparent.

128
00:11:45,379 --> 00:11:47,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what is the true gratification in the desire?

129
00:11:48,141 --> 00:11:49,141
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the true danger?

130
00:11:49,281 --> 00:11:50,302
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the true escape?

131
00:11:51,683 --> 00:11:53,484
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that's the only meditation that Buddha praised.

132
00:11:55,513 --> 00:11:59,574
[SPEAKER_01]: And that withdrawal from sensuality is synonymous for jhāna.

133
00:11:59,974 --> 00:12:02,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, jhāna is the result of your withdrawal from sensuality.

134
00:12:03,135 --> 00:12:14,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, I don't think it hurts to re-emphasize, when I say withdrawal, it doesn't mean like you shut your door, pull your blinds, and currently you don't have any desire coming in your senses.

135
00:12:14,239 --> 00:12:18,700
[SPEAKER_01]: It means internally surmounting it with your mind by understanding for what it is.

136
00:12:19,501 --> 00:12:23,022
[SPEAKER_01]: People, most people are just driven around by their desires because they don't have a clue.

137
00:12:23,860 --> 00:12:26,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Why or how and to what extent they are controlled by them?

138
00:12:27,101 --> 00:12:29,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first thing you need to do is say no to them.

139
00:12:29,762 --> 00:12:33,083
[SPEAKER_01]: And then endure that pain and then eventually you might be able to understand the nature of it.

140
00:12:33,563 --> 00:12:35,203
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it meant you surmounted.

141
00:12:35,743 --> 00:12:37,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what it describes, that sutta.

142
00:12:37,844 --> 00:12:38,344
[SPEAKER_01]: You carry on.

143
00:12:38,624 --> 00:12:43,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, read those, what it says, when it says the Buddha, the meditation that the Buddha did not praise.

144
00:12:45,706 --> 00:12:52,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not, not, eh... Having not abandoned the sensuality,

145
00:12:53,509 --> 00:13:00,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Still being liable to sensuality, not knowing the escape from that sensuality, he meditates.

146
00:13:01,589 --> 00:13:03,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And he calls himself a meditator and so on.

147
00:13:04,371 --> 00:13:05,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the meditation.

148
00:13:05,452 --> 00:13:23,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unless you have surmounted, abandoned, understood the escape from sensuality, by the way, not from particular sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, the nature of sense desire that you are liable to, you're not liable to anymore, that's your right meditation and that's the necessary condition for jhāna.

149
00:13:23,205 --> 00:13:24,666
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not even jhāna, yet.

150
00:13:26,555 --> 00:13:27,336
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a pre-course.

151
00:13:27,716 --> 00:13:28,837
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no jhāna without it.

152
00:13:29,097 --> 00:13:31,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And practicing that will have to result in jhāna.

153
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:35,121
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot result in anything else.

154
00:13:35,721 --> 00:13:43,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why when the Buddha said in other suttas, in more concise ways, when he would describe what is the right meditation, he would just describe practice of jhāna.

155
00:13:44,326 --> 00:13:49,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it already implies a thorough withdrawal from sense domain, thorough abandonment of sensual desire.

156
00:13:50,749 --> 00:13:56,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Which probably undoes, like, 85% of all the wrong views that people carry are based on their sensuality.

157
00:13:56,813 --> 00:13:57,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

158
00:13:59,015 --> 00:14:03,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, sensuality is called, like, weakener, the hindrances in sensuality, weakening of wisdom.

159
00:14:04,339 --> 00:14:08,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, without sensuality, people will much quicker understand the nature of things.

160
00:14:08,622 --> 00:14:10,383
[SPEAKER_01]: You get the right view, be fully liberated.

161
00:14:10,984 --> 00:14:16,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the sensuality that keeps you bound with your own ignorance, through your own choices of binding yourself, basically.

162
00:14:20,038 --> 00:14:24,359
[SPEAKER_00]: And that sensuality, in essence, is the valuing of pleasure.

163
00:14:24,419 --> 00:14:24,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

164
00:14:24,659 --> 00:14:25,919
[SPEAKER_01]: We said that in other talks.

165
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:29,220
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, I have this raging desire or something.

166
00:14:29,260 --> 00:14:29,480
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

167
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:31,581
[SPEAKER_01]: It's way, way more general than that.

168
00:14:31,781 --> 00:14:33,501
[SPEAKER_01]: More, more primordial than that.

169
00:14:34,062 --> 00:14:47,645
[SPEAKER_01]: It's literally an attitude of you gratuitously maintaining value in experience of pleasure of any kind.

170
00:14:50,092 --> 00:14:54,954
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you value pleasure for no reason.

171
00:14:55,834 --> 00:14:58,015
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no reason to value pleasure more than pain.

172
00:14:58,955 --> 00:15:00,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why is pleasure valuable?

173
00:15:01,416 --> 00:15:03,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply because it's pleasant.

174
00:15:05,077 --> 00:15:16,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pleasure of... the pleasure, basically, confuses you into valuing the pleasure as this thing that's worthy to develop, to protect, to gain, to increase.

175
00:15:18,221 --> 00:15:19,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't value the pleasure,

176
00:15:21,672 --> 00:15:31,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you would not basically be subject to that perpetual desire that is then seeking that pleasure as the goal.

177
00:15:32,304 --> 00:15:42,075
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know, again, it can be pleasure of a sexual intercourse, or food, or simply some lovely peaceful music, or a beautiful sunset vista and a cool breeze.

178
00:15:43,137 --> 00:15:49,305
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're seeking that out for the sake of pleasure on account of your body, on account of your senses, it's sensual pleasure.

179
00:15:50,186 --> 00:15:56,514
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't seek it out on account of that pleasure of your body, then it's just agreeable.

180
00:15:57,114 --> 00:15:59,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the pleasure of sensuality.

181
00:16:00,866 --> 00:16:13,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, yes, you can sit on top of a mountain and admire the view, for sensual reasons, or not for sensual reasons, if you basically have abandoned that gratuitous value of pleasure being valuable in itself.

182
00:16:14,117 --> 00:16:16,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, certain things, certain acts must stop.

183
00:16:16,598 --> 00:16:20,782
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot possibly do them without the value of sensuality, such as sexual integrals.

184
00:16:21,903 --> 00:16:27,207
[SPEAKER_01]: As we said, that's impossible to do without already being within the perception of that sensual value.

185
00:16:28,963 --> 00:16:35,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, engaging in such acts means you're already implying the value of that, gratuitous value of that pleasure in general.

186
00:16:37,228 --> 00:16:44,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you go around living your life, existing, and on the level of that existence, pleasure is the value for you.

187
00:16:47,375 --> 00:16:52,698
[SPEAKER_01]: You will then be going left and right, seeking things that will provide you with pleasure, because you value it.

188
00:16:53,941 --> 00:17:01,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Naturally, if pleasure is on your mind, so whatever you do is for the sake of experiencing pleasure, because that's your goal, by having that value.

189
00:17:02,043 --> 00:17:02,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

190
00:17:02,483 --> 00:17:06,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So that value that you carry is before you engage with sense objects.

191
00:17:06,884 --> 00:17:12,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're liable to the desire towards sense objects, because you actually carry on that lack of perspective.

192
00:17:12,845 --> 00:17:13,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You value pleasure.

193
00:17:14,386 --> 00:17:16,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Valuing the pleasure is the result of the lack of perspective.

194
00:17:17,606 --> 00:17:20,527
[SPEAKER_01]: If you, however, develop perspective on the value of pleasure,

195
00:17:21,521 --> 00:17:24,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that it's completely not valuable at all.

196
00:17:24,843 --> 00:17:27,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean, oh, now I need to experience pain.

197
00:17:27,144 --> 00:17:27,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

198
00:17:27,684 --> 00:17:28,645
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the other extreme.

199
00:17:29,665 --> 00:17:32,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Just means, I must remove the value of feeling of pleasure.

200
00:17:33,867 --> 00:17:35,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what is valuable?

201
00:17:35,488 --> 00:17:36,388
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing valuable.

202
00:17:36,488 --> 00:17:42,471
[SPEAKER_01]: You just assume that it is, and you live your whole life according to it.

203
00:17:42,491 --> 00:17:46,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feeling is not... it's... it comes and goes.

204
00:17:46,633 --> 00:17:47,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mine.

205
00:17:47,874 --> 00:17:48,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

206
00:17:49,659 --> 00:17:56,887
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I taste agreeable food, I recognize it as agreeable, but because I don't have the value of pleasure...

207
00:17:57,768 --> 00:18:00,009
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not have desire for more of that.

208
00:18:00,510 --> 00:18:09,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's the more general context of valuing the pleasure that then determines how much pleasure you get, and as in, then creates the discrepancy for wanting more.

209
00:18:10,035 --> 00:18:14,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the value of pleasure, it's on one level, and sense objects and pleasures you get from it, it's on another.

210
00:18:15,219 --> 00:18:17,140
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the desire is insatiable.

211
00:18:17,160 --> 00:18:20,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're looking, basically, for it, where it isn't.

212
00:18:21,042 --> 00:18:23,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Your desire is that value of pleasure.

213
00:18:23,405 --> 00:18:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha said, that delight in pleasure, as in, I delight in the prospect of experiencing agreeable sense objects and feelings.

214
00:18:33,155 --> 00:18:35,697
[SPEAKER_01]: In general, I delight in that prospect.

215
00:18:36,418 --> 00:18:38,139
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the gratification of my pleasure.

216
00:18:38,459 --> 00:18:39,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else is secondary.

217
00:18:39,919 --> 00:18:56,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you eating the cake, or admiring the vista, or chasing down relationships, or whatever, for the sake of sensual gratification, all that is secondary to you already gratifying your pleasure by allowing that value to control you, by maintaining the value of pleasure in itself.

218
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:04,788
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, what happens, or what could happen if you stop maintaining the value of pleasure?

219
00:19:04,928 --> 00:19:06,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that mean you would go in pain?

220
00:19:10,756 --> 00:19:11,197
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it doesn't.

221
00:19:12,218 --> 00:19:15,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you ask yourself now, do I value pleasure?

222
00:19:16,397 --> 00:19:18,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm not engaging with anything sensual.

223
00:19:18,138 --> 00:19:19,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can drop that value.

224
00:19:19,659 --> 00:19:23,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can say, I don't... you can actually drop it.

225
00:19:23,942 --> 00:19:25,783
[SPEAKER_01]: You can drop it, and then you forget about it.

226
00:19:26,203 --> 00:19:26,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the point.

227
00:19:26,964 --> 00:19:27,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

228
00:19:27,504 --> 00:19:31,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you keep reminding yourself, and the more you dwell on it, the clearer it becomes.

229
00:19:31,467 --> 00:19:33,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point of understanding.

230
00:19:33,148 --> 00:19:36,790
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of understanding is, you make the effort to clarify things that need to be understood.

231
00:19:37,631 --> 00:19:41,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once that perspective becomes thoroughly understood, you can't drop it.

232
00:19:42,053 --> 00:19:44,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't forget about it, in that sense.

233
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:54,445
[SPEAKER_01]: But what I'm trying to say is that now, when people would think, oh, if I abandon value of pleasure, it's implicitly assumed, oh, that means everything will be painful.

234
00:19:54,665 --> 00:19:55,145
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

235
00:19:56,066 --> 00:20:03,150
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is just ask yourself now, when you are not surrounded by sense objects, you are neither in pain nor in pleasure now.

236
00:20:04,210 --> 00:20:08,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, things are a bit rough in this jungle, but we are used to it, so it doesn't bother us.

237
00:20:09,253 --> 00:20:10,394
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are no other things of

238
00:20:11,638 --> 00:20:13,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable or pleasant nature inciting either.

239
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:19,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you ask yourself, okay, so in this sort of neutral environment, am I liable to sensuality?

240
00:20:20,162 --> 00:20:27,026
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, if things change, if things become more comfortable, more agreeable, will the sense desire arise in me?

241
00:20:27,606 --> 00:20:36,390
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you say, if you recognize that it will, means, okay, so I still value experiencing pleasure at some point, sometime.

242
00:20:36,570 --> 00:20:37,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

243
00:20:37,371 --> 00:20:37,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

244
00:20:38,291 --> 00:20:44,254
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize that value that exists right here, right now, is actually existing on account of this neutral feeling.

245
00:20:46,475 --> 00:20:50,397
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not engaging in pleasure now, that reminds you of pleasure, that there you want more pleasure.

246
00:20:51,357 --> 00:20:53,318
[SPEAKER_01]: You're quite withdrawn from pleasure now.

247
00:20:53,378 --> 00:20:59,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, somehow, the mind could still value centrality on account of which you have the future sense desire.

248
00:21:00,321 --> 00:21:01,242
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no value,

249
00:21:02,148 --> 00:21:05,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Of that pleasure in general, future sense desire cannot manifest in you.

250
00:21:06,651 --> 00:21:10,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why don't you abandon that value right here, right now, on account of this neutral feeling?

251
00:21:11,554 --> 00:21:20,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Consequently, by actually gratuitously maintaining that value of pleasure, this neutral feeling is actually experienced painfully.

252
00:21:22,302 --> 00:21:23,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you value pleasure.

253
00:21:23,362 --> 00:21:24,143
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your threshold.

254
00:21:24,163 --> 00:21:25,864
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your gratuitous criteria.

255
00:21:27,187 --> 00:21:33,911
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha said, when you don't recognize the neutral feeling that's here, it's going to be felt as unpleasant.

256
00:21:35,012 --> 00:21:38,014
[SPEAKER_01]: When you recognize it, it's going to be felt as pleasant.

257
00:21:38,555 --> 00:21:41,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And that pleasure of neutral feeling is a good type of pleasure.

258
00:21:41,597 --> 00:21:43,578
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a pleasure you cannot crave for.

259
00:21:43,598 --> 00:21:47,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, it's not rooted, it's not determined by this value of sensuality.

260
00:21:47,761 --> 00:21:48,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I see.

261
00:21:48,401 --> 00:21:53,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it sounds to me like you drop the value, here and now, of pleasure.

262
00:21:55,107 --> 00:21:55,908
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want that.

263
00:21:55,948 --> 00:21:57,248
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need that anymore.

264
00:21:57,768 --> 00:21:58,509
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't value it.

265
00:21:58,529 --> 00:22:00,610
[SPEAKER_00]: It hasn't given me any lasting anything.

266
00:22:00,810 --> 00:22:07,213
[SPEAKER_00]: It hasn't given me enough anything that I can resort to depend on.

267
00:22:07,253 --> 00:22:11,756
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you mean, you allow that idea to endure, to possess, to protect it.

268
00:22:12,316 --> 00:22:13,016
[SPEAKER_01]: You cultivate it.

269
00:22:13,036 --> 00:22:14,077
[SPEAKER_01]: You think about it further.

270
00:22:14,177 --> 00:22:16,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, see, now you drop it.

271
00:22:16,198 --> 00:22:17,379
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand it hasn't provided...

272
00:22:17,399 --> 00:22:20,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have all that essential thoughts come in your mind,

273
00:22:21,330 --> 00:22:24,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but I can remind myself, it's not valuable.

274
00:22:24,811 --> 00:22:30,994
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I realize that, if I really realize that, the burden of the value of getting...

275
00:22:31,434 --> 00:22:35,235
[SPEAKER_00]: I must... Oh, yeah, you feel the burden drop.

276
00:22:35,455 --> 00:22:36,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Gone.

277
00:22:36,496 --> 00:22:37,336
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel the release.

278
00:22:37,356 --> 00:22:37,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

279
00:22:37,776 --> 00:22:40,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that release is that neutral feeling.

280
00:22:41,258 --> 00:22:41,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

281
00:22:41,598 --> 00:22:42,858
[SPEAKER_00]: You're released into peace.

282
00:22:43,518 --> 00:22:47,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Which you would have... you always had in front of you, but you were not recognizing it.

283
00:22:48,609 --> 00:22:49,389
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

284
00:22:49,410 --> 00:23:00,616
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when you have a thought, say, okay, you do have, on some physical level, you're restrained, and you recognize, I'll never engage with this anymore, it never provides any satisfaction, it's just burden, further disturbance.

285
00:23:01,036 --> 00:23:01,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

286
00:23:01,376 --> 00:23:02,237
[SPEAKER_01]: You're established within that.

287
00:23:02,597 --> 00:23:07,180
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, on a more subtler level, your mind might have a thought or something about sensuality.

288
00:23:07,560 --> 00:23:08,500
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I must not think that.

289
00:23:08,560 --> 00:23:08,700
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

290
00:23:09,161 --> 00:23:12,803
[SPEAKER_01]: When that thought arises, include that thought in this context.

291
00:23:13,283 --> 00:23:15,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't forget the context on account of the thought.

292
00:23:15,364 --> 00:23:24,768
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now I have these thoughts, but this value remains un... this, sorry, the new value of devalued sensuality remains unchanged.

293
00:23:24,788 --> 00:23:34,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, allow thoughts to endure, sensual thoughts, if they manifested, allow them to endure against the backdrop of devalued sensuality, because that's how you devalue the thoughts as well.

294
00:23:36,595 --> 00:23:45,420
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you forget about the perspective, and go ahead and deal with the thoughts, and get rid of the thoughts, and oppress the thoughts, and not think about the thoughts, and duh-duh-duh-duh-duh-duh-duh-duh... Ah, well, they hook you.

295
00:23:45,941 --> 00:23:50,744
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not go physically and engage with sensuality, but you are revolving around it by fighting the thoughts.

296
00:23:51,664 --> 00:24:04,152
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you just want to not lose that right perspective, yoniso, manasikāra, even when the... whether it's the physical prospect, verbal prospect, mental prospect, the deep values of sensuality as the new value

297
00:24:06,946 --> 00:24:16,111
[SPEAKER_01]: And the longer you maintain it and protect it, the more of anything like... like the discrepancy caused by sensual desire will burn away.

298
00:24:16,151 --> 00:24:16,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Scorch.

299
00:24:16,511 --> 00:24:18,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Buddha... what was the simile?

300
00:24:18,052 --> 00:24:20,173
[SPEAKER_01]: The... the heat up... heated up pan.

301
00:24:20,513 --> 00:24:24,935
[SPEAKER_01]: You throw and instantly the water will evaporate, if it's really, really hot.

302
00:24:25,716 --> 00:24:26,436
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your pan.

303
00:24:26,496 --> 00:24:29,258
[SPEAKER_01]: The hot pan is that new value of devalued sensuality.

304
00:24:29,758 --> 00:24:33,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter what sensual object come in your sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, thought.

305
00:24:34,598 --> 00:24:39,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Evaporates instantly, because that's... you don't stop it to deal with it, you just maintain the context.

306
00:24:40,144 --> 00:24:40,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It's valueless.

307
00:24:41,105 --> 00:24:45,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Or another simile of eating the food and not catching the bait, like the deer.

308
00:24:48,512 --> 00:24:51,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you not take the food that has the bait?

309
00:24:51,555 --> 00:24:52,676
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't forget about the bait.

310
00:24:53,317 --> 00:24:58,482
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the bait, but you know the bait, and you get to recognize where the bait is, and you don't eat that food.

311
00:24:59,513 --> 00:25:04,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you go around and eat everything else you want, except the one that would hook you.

312
00:25:04,876 --> 00:25:13,722
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can then have plenty of actions aimed at, oh, I'll lay down because my back hurts, as the Buddha would say, or, oh, this is agreeable grove, let us spend some time here.

313
00:25:14,582 --> 00:25:18,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, for Pratyūjana, non-free from sensuality, you would think, oh, but the Buddha has desires.

314
00:25:21,797 --> 00:25:23,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, not the desires that you think of.

315
00:25:24,239 --> 00:25:27,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you start the practice, you're fully within centrality.

316
00:25:27,421 --> 00:25:33,146
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have a neutral point from which you can see arahant type of desire and putujana type of desire.

317
00:25:33,266 --> 00:25:34,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the putujana type of desire.

318
00:25:35,948 --> 00:25:42,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you can do is work your way out from the inside of that desire and then what you have is not a desire.

319
00:25:43,874 --> 00:25:49,378
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, your intention can still value avoiding discomfort, for example, and it will not be central.

320
00:25:50,552 --> 00:26:14,478
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless you're free, if you're not free from sensuality in that fundamental manner, as the Buddha said, you don't know the liability, the gratification liability, danger and escape from it, then even the innocent intentions towards agreeable, your dwelling, your sleeping place, your food will be thoroughly sensual, because you made it so.

321
00:26:22,637 --> 00:26:25,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the danger is that value.

322
00:26:26,939 --> 00:26:27,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

323
00:26:27,319 --> 00:26:33,142
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger is not like, oh, see, the danger is if I go out and seek things out, and then things can go not the way I want them to.

324
00:26:33,463 --> 00:26:34,303
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all the result.

325
00:26:35,444 --> 00:26:37,905
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger is implicit before you engage with it.

326
00:26:37,945 --> 00:26:40,767
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, gratification is implicit before you engage with it.

327
00:26:41,087 --> 00:26:44,148
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the escape is implicit before you engage with it.

328
00:26:44,168 --> 00:26:45,429
[SPEAKER_00]: But gratification is the danger.

329
00:26:46,029 --> 00:26:46,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Well,

330
00:26:47,495 --> 00:26:49,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, danger is within the gratification, exactly.

331
00:26:49,635 --> 00:26:52,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratification is the aspect of the same thing that's dangerous.

332
00:26:53,976 --> 00:27:01,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing the gratification as the same aspect of the danger is how you get the escape from it.

333
00:27:02,878 --> 00:27:11,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So the... just that value, just that happiness, that delight, as the Buddha would say, on account of valuing the pleasure, that's the gratification of the pleasure.

334
00:27:12,059 --> 00:27:14,440
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the extent of it.

335
00:27:14,460 --> 00:27:15,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, what is the danger?

336
00:27:16,453 --> 00:27:23,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that utterly, like virtually, almost abstract value, and it's abstract because you never stop.

337
00:27:23,457 --> 00:27:24,437
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you're never restrained.

338
00:27:24,477 --> 00:27:28,279
[SPEAKER_01]: You never step back from the sense objects to get perspective on them.

339
00:27:29,059 --> 00:27:31,941
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, second, you never think about, wait, why do I value pleasure?

340
00:27:32,721 --> 00:27:34,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why do I move from discomfort into comfort?

341
00:27:36,003 --> 00:27:36,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, let me try.

342
00:27:36,523 --> 00:27:37,844
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sometimes the Buddha would say, well,

343
00:27:39,009 --> 00:27:41,330
[SPEAKER_01]: They would ask him, or should you do these ascetic practices?

344
00:27:41,350 --> 00:27:47,973
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, well, let those who know that those practices would bring the wholesome to increase, unwholesome to decrease, let them do it.

345
00:27:48,873 --> 00:27:57,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes that means, let me go in the adverse enduring of the elements and hardship, because I recognize that as devaluing, automatic devaluing of sensuality.

346
00:27:57,196 --> 00:27:58,057
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not a guarantee.

347
00:27:58,717 --> 00:28:02,939
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know where the value is, and what, like, oh, this value is gratuitous.

348
00:28:03,499 --> 00:28:05,780
[SPEAKER_01]: I must basically challenge that value internally.

349
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:13,743
[SPEAKER_01]: And that does not necessarily mean that everybody can do the same practice externally in terms of ascetic practices and endurance and so on.

350
00:28:14,123 --> 00:28:15,104
[SPEAKER_01]: To some extent, yes.

351
00:28:15,684 --> 00:28:20,046
[SPEAKER_01]: But to what extent, how far you need to take that, that depends on... well, depends on the individual.

352
00:28:22,667 --> 00:28:30,430
